@page "/docs/tools/vs-code-properties"

@* The Properties view, Set an Option and the hover (0.9.4: out/inspector.js, setOption.js). Reading runs izzy
   inspect and changing runs izzy set, both with a free account: the same tiers as pricing's row
   "Edit options and markup in the IDE". *@

<ArticlePage Section="tools" Slug="vs-code-properties">
    <Lead>
        Properties lists every option of the component under the cursor, with the values its tag sets. Use
        it to find an option without leaving the file, and to set one without typing its name. Seeing the
        options and setting them needs a free account.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Open it</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Click a component's tag in a <Code>.razor</Code> page. Properties opens in the side bar on the right,
            beside your code, and follows the cursor from then on. <Kbd Keys="Ctrl+Alt+P" /> opens it from the
            keyboard (<Kbd Keys="Cmd+Option+P" /> on a Mac).
            <Anchor Href="docs/tools/vs-code#settings"><Code>izzyblazor.inspector.open</Code></Anchor> changes
            when it opens by itself.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In VS Code before 1.106, and in editors with no side bar on the right for extensions, Properties is in
            the IzzyBlazor side bar. You can drag it anywhere.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Read a component's options</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>The breadcrumbs show where the component sits, such as Container, Column, DataGrid. Click one to see that component's options.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Options are in the groups of the component's docs page, such as Layout, Field and Styling.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>An option the tag sets has a bar in the margin. An option it doesn't set shows its default.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A long group shows the options in use and the main ones. Show more shows the rest.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Point to an option, or move focus to it, to read its description, type and default at the bottom of the panel.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The filter box finds an option by name. The toggle at its end shows only the options the tag sets.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A value written as a C# expression is shown as written.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">Change an option</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Pick a value or type one for an option that is on or off, a choice, text or a number.
            Other options show their type, and are set in the code. The change is written to the open editor at once, so undo takes it
            back, and the file is saved only when you save it. To go back to the default, choose Default in a
            list, clear a text field, or use Reset beside the option's name.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            The <Anchor Href="docs/tools/vs-code-preview">live preview</Anchor> shows a change when the file is
            saved: <Anchor Href="docs/tools/vs-code#settings"><Code>izzyblazor.inspector.saveAfterChange</Code></Anchor>
            saves after each change.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Without Properties</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Set an Option (<Kbd Keys="Ctrl+Alt+O" />, or <Kbd Keys="Ctrl+." /> on a tag) lists the options of
            the component under the cursor that it can set, the ones in use first. Pick one, then its value.
            It asks in VS Code's own lists at the top of the window.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Point to a component's name in the file to see what it is and the options its tag sets, with links to
            set one or to open Properties.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>
